<node id="Comparative Analysis of Layer Types in Sequence Transduction (table)">
  <data key="d0">Comparative Analysis of Layer Types in Sequence Transduction (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">This table compares the computational complexity, sequential operations, and maximum path lengths of self-attention, recurrent, and convolutional layers. It underscores the efficiency of self-attention in handling long-range dependencies, aligning with the surrounding content's emphasis on the advantages of multi-head attention in the Transformer architecture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0ceece0f3be34f4dce5de239281aab5a</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763840653</data>
</node>
<node id="Positional Encoding in Transformers (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Positional Encoding in Transformers (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">This equation defines the sinusoidal positional encodings used in the Transformer model to inject information about token positions, enabling the model to maintain sequence order and learn relationships between tokens effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c07c2be588c5ac380f3be897738d6a00</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763840653</data>
</node>
